*We must comply with GDPR*
{panel}

Any request for erasure of personal data submitted by a driver must be treated as a Data Subject Request (DSR) and handled in line with our internal global process. Under GDPR, we cannot reject these requests automatically or unilaterally without a valid legal basis. This applies even if the driver was blocked or previously engaged in abuse or fraud.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*We* *_can_* *retain certain data lawfully*
{panel}

In Romania, GEO 49/2019 Art. 25(e) requires transport platforms to retain specific driver data for at least 2 years after deactivation. Separately, GDPR permits us to retain data where we have a legal obligation or legitimate interest (e.g. fraud prevention, dispute resolution, tax compliance). However, this needs to be communicated clearly to the data subject as part of the deletion response.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*What we do in practice*
{panel}

When a driver requests deletion:

# We mark the account as _“deleted”_ in Admin (we do *not* change status to "DELETED").
# We send the standard GDPR deletion response template from the KB, stating that the request was granted but some data will be retained due to legal and operational requirements.
# We retain the data flagged for legal hold, fraud prevention, or required under local law (e.g. tax authority reporting). This is compliant with GDPR *as long as we explain it properly*.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Avoid reactivation by abuse*
{panel}

Keeping the “deleted” status, along with duplicate-checks in place, mitigates the risk of drivers creating “clean” new accounts to bypass blocks or gain referral bonuses. The data we retain allows us to detect and prevent this.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Action for this type of cases*
{panel}

The driver’s request should be formally handled as a DSR. We should contact the fleet and either:

# Ask them to have the driver submit a deletion request directly through the DSR form, or
# Obtain written authorisation that the fleet is acting on the driver’s behalf.

Once that’s received:

* CS to mark the account as “deleted” in Admin.
* Use the standard GDPR deletion template response (see KB).
* Retain data strictly as needed to comply with GEO 49/2019 and internal legal holds (e.g. for fraud).

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Going forward*
{panel}

All teams (Ops, CS, and Privacy) must be aligned and follow the global DSR process. Any exceptions or changes to this process must be reviewed and approved by the Privacy Team to avoid compliance risk.



❗ User Insights and Privacy Team, together with us, will be working on data deletion/retention rules during 2025-2026. We will ensure that local rules are included to the retention schedule, to make sure that the data is retained in line with the requirements of local law.

