Hi [~accountid:63fe21787c30bbd6b33e09d3], when you have a moment please could you sense-check my draft response to Marija below? In particular, do you agree with my response at Q3 below, or do you think we should be sending email communications? Thank you! 🙌 

----

Hi [~accountid:712020:0ef4b6b0-5fb9-4a66-a66b-0a7c707a4f8f], thank you for setting out the below - very helpful. For context, the piece that I am working on looks at both anomalies and fraud, so our advice and recommendations will be covered in the memo once finalised. In the meantime, I have set out below our responses to your questions in turn in {color:#006644}green{color}.  

Hi [~accountid:712020:238a1b89-0f1b-4797-9066-90af4142a510] , the questions we have for Bolt Boost fraud/suspicious cases related to *both* anomalies and fraud. We will set up rules in the fraud engine based on the patterns that we found and we will be flagging orders which are anomalies. For more details on the exact scenarios refer to [+the following document+|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/COMM/pages/6098878465/UK+Driver+Benefits+-+Driver+Abuse+update+20+03+25]. 
*Questions for legal team*

* Q1: Relates to the first condition you stated: “*_Conduct a thorough review_*_: Confirm that fraud has occurred, not just a suspicion._”.
** What exactly do we mean by *review*? In the case of national living wage compensation abuse, we will be looking at anomalies in the driver journey (e.g. distance between logged and estimated pick up time, estimated and logged trip duration). We will not have a human reviewer of each flag. The flagging of those transactions would be done by the fraud engine. We will send the flag to billing. Billing will recalculate the NLW compensation based on fraud signals. No human review is included in the process. {color:#006644}*Legal Team Response:*{color} {color:#006644}a thorough review means that (a) we must ensure that the fraud engine carries out enough checks to ensure that we have a substantial degree of certainty that fraud has indeed occurred, and (b) we have as much documented evidence as possible from that review in order to substantiate and justify the potential blocking of a driver/non-reimbursement in the event that the driver disputes the decision. Unless the fraud engine review is robust and error-free, some form of human review (in addition to the fraud engine review) would be the ideal scenario in order to ensure that any errors or nuances that the fraud engine may miss are detected. {color}
** In majority of these cases we cannot say with absolute certainty that driver intended to commit fraud. It could be the case that driver forgot to end the trip and instead of logging 15 minutes as it was predicted for that distance, the driver ended the trip after 3 hours.  We can say that it’s suspicious that the trip lasted 12x times longer than it should have, flag the transaction and exclude it from the NLW compensation calculation. For more details on the exact scenarios refer to [+the following document+|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/COMM/pages/6098878465/UK+Driver+Benefits+-+Driver+Abuse+update+20+03+25]. {color:#006644}*Legal Team Response:*{color} {color:#006644}noted, thank you. {color}
* Q2: When we send this flag of a suspicious trip to billing, should they _exclude_ this ride from the NLW compensation calculation, or should they include the ride but use estimated values (estimated pick up, estimated ride duration and distance)? {color:#006644}*Legal Team Response:*{color} {color:#006644}clause 19.14 of the {color}[{color:#006644}driver terms and conditions{color}|https://bolt.eu/en-gb/legal/tnc-general-terms-drivers/?category=rides]{color:#006644} and clause 4.4 of the {color}[{color:#006644}fraud policy{color}|https://bolt.eu/en-gb/legal/fraud-prevention-policy/?category=other]{color:#006644} (which are both incorporated into the {color}[{color:#006644}Bolt Boost terms and conditions{color}|https://bolt.eu/en-gb/legal/tnc-bolt-boost/?category=rides]{color:#006644}) state that where we reasonably suspect that a driver has not complied with the fraud policy, Bolt is under no obligation to make or facilitate any payments for such time as this is investigated. On that basis, we are comfortable that any suspicious trip may be excluded from the NLW compensation calculation whilst a suspicious trip is being investigated. {color}
** Excluding the flagged trip from NLW compensation calculation would be a much easier solution for our development teams. 
** If we need to use estimated values, this would mean more work for Billing Rides development team.
* Q3: Once the correction from Q2 is done, should there be some email communication to the driver? Right now we do not send them an email with details of the total NLW compensation amount. Do we need to let the driver know of national living wage compensation correction (rides we did not take into account)? {color:#006644}*Legal Team Response:*{color} {color:#006644}although in this scenario, we note that you do not block or suspend drivers, we understand that communications are sent out to the driver in the event of suspected fraud by way of a “Statement of Reason” (clause 5.3 of the {color}[{color:#006644}fraud policy{color}|https://bolt.eu/en-gb/legal/fraud-prevention-policy/?category=other]{color:#006644}) and, if a decision is made to terminate a driver’s access to the platform, the driver has 30 days within which to appeal - {color}{color:#006644}+please let us know if this is not the case in practice+{color}{color:#006644}. On the basis that (a) drivers will receive communication in the event of suspected fraud that needs to be investigated and (b) at present, our terms and conditions and fraud policy does not require Bolt to provide a breakdown of the NLW compensation, we are comfortable with the business maintaining the current position of not sending emails with the NLW compensation correction. The risk, however, is that drivers may contact Bolt and query any discrepancies in the NLW compensation, which will need to be responded to. Referring back to our comments at Q1 above, it is therefore paramount that we have written evidence of (a) the reasons for any NLW compensation correction and (b) how such compensation correct has been calculated.  {color}
** Note: We will not block or suspend drivers. All we need to do is control which transactions are included in the NLW top up compensation. If we identify a large anomaly, we want to be able to ignore those trips from the top up calculation. Drivers will still be paid for the ride fare. {color:#006644}*Legal Team Response:*{color} {color:#006644}noted, thank you. Please see our response to Q3 above.  {color}

I hope the above is helpful, but do let us know if you have any further queries. 

Thank you

