Hi [~accountid:712020:f2473ea6-a01f-4207-919c-160dbe10a686],

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
We can provide the additional requested coupon ride data, which includes the starting and ending points of these codes' journeys, as well as the time of each journey.

However, this is subject to the condition that the information shared is strictly necessary for a clear and justifiable reason - in this case, it pertains to fraud prevention by the business customer.
{panel}

*Detailed explanation:*

I agree that our standard approach is to resist such requests due to the following reasons:
# *Business Considerations:* Collecting this data on an ad hoc basis requires significant effort from our operations team.
# *Privacy Considerations:* We adhere to the principle of data minimisation, aiming to share as little information as possible with business customers. Additional data is only shared when there is a clear and justifiable reason, such as fraud prevention in this instance.
That said, ad hoc we can substantiate the sharing of additional data with business customers if there is a clear and legitimate reason. We have a clause in our BB Terms and Conditions that permits data transfer, and we inform our users through our privacy notices that when an order is paid by someone else (i.e., a business customer), they can access their ride details, including in cases involving coupons and promo codes. However, this functionality is not part of the standard BB offerings, such as when a business payment method is used with a card.

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
I believe a very similar issue was escalated to me recently, where I outlined the associated risks. I think it might have been the same situation with McDonald's in Lithuania, and the privacy team communicated with your colleague.
{panel}

